# 🐧 Linux Fundamentals

## Navigating the Filesystem
Linux uses a hierarchical filesystem shaped like a tree.

Root directory: /  
Home directory: ~  
Current directory:  
pwd

### Navigation Commands
ls              # List contents  
ls -a           # Show hidden files  
cd folder       # Enter folder  
cd ..           # Up one level  
cd ~            # Home directory  
mkdir newdir    # Create directory  
rmdir newdir    # Remove empty directory

### Absolute vs Relative Paths
Absolute: cd /home/user/project  
Relative: cd project

---

## Creating Files & Redirecting Output
touch file.txt  
echo "Hello" > file.txt  
echo "More" >> file.txt  

Prevent overwrites:  
set -o noclobber  
echo "Force" >| file.txt

---

## Hidden Files
echo "data" > .hiddenfile  
ls -a  
Used for configuration files like .bashrc.

---

## File Permissions
r = read  
w = write  
x = execute  

Example: rw-rw-r--  
Owner: rw  
Group: rw  
Others: r

### Numeric System
read = 4  
write = 2  
execute = 1  

700 = rwx --- ---  
Owner full access; others none.

### Symbolic System
chmod u+rwx file  
chmod g-w file  
chmod o=rx file  
chmod -R 755 directory/

---

## Ownership — chown
chown user file  
chown user:group file  
chown -R user:group directory/

---

## Bash & Shell Basics
#!/bin/bash  
sh — classic shell  
zsh — modern shell  
python — script interpreter

---

## Recursive Flags
-R = recursive  
-r = depends on command  
rm -r → recursive delete  
ls -r → reverse order

---

## Listing Files
ls  
ls -l  
ls -a  
ls -la  
ls -l dir

